WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.801
Your code reads Limits.CAP.

2
00:00:04.801 --> 00:00:07.447
The JVM has never seen this class.

3
00:00:07.447 --> 00:00:09.781
Its bytes sit unread in a jar.

4
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:15.420
A class loader finds a class's bytes and turns them into a Class.

5
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:16.727
The JDK has three.

6
00:00:16.727 --> 00:00:18.616
Bootstrap loads java.base.

7
00:00:18.616 --> 00:00:21.303
Platform loads the other JDK modules.

8
00:00:21.303 --> 00:00:24.280
And the app loader loads your class path.

9
00:00:24.433 --> 00:00:26.416
The app loader gets the request.

10
00:00:26.416 --> 00:00:29.204
Before it looks anywhere, it asks its parent.

11
00:00:29.204 --> 00:00:30.939
So does the platform loader.

12
00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:32.773
Bootstrap has no Limits.

13
00:00:32.773 --> 00:00:34.480
Platform has none either.

14
00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.032
Only then does the app loader search the class path.

15
00:00:38.032 --> 00:00:41.379
It finds Limits in app.jar and defines the class.

16
00:00:42.167 --> 00:00:44.471
This is parent-first delegation.

17
00:00:44.471 --> 00:00:47.640
Ask for String, and bootstrap answers first.

18
00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.592
So a stray jar can't replace a JDK class.

19
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:55.153
The loader that defines a class is part of its identity.

20
00:00:55.153 --> 00:00:58.833
At run time, a class is its name plus its defining loader.

21
00:00:58.833 --> 00:01:03.972
So two loaders that define Limits from the same bytes make two different classes.

22
00:01:04.867 --> 00:01:08.331
Defining the class was loading, the first of three phases.

23
00:01:08.331 --> 00:01:11.557
It made a Class object, and none of your code has run.

24
00:01:11.733 --> 00:01:13.045
Linking comes next.

25
00:01:13.045 --> 00:01:16.013
It verifies that the bytecode is type safe.

26
00:01:16.233 --> 00:01:19.772
It prepares the static fields with default values.

27
00:01:19.772 --> 00:01:22.391
So CAP exists now, and it holds null.

28
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.468
And it resolves names in the constant pool, lazily.

29
00:01:26.468 --> 00:01:29.935
A class that uses a deleted Helper class runs fine.

30
00:01:29.935 --> 00:01:34.967
It throws NoClassDefFoundError only when the branch that uses Helper runs.

31
00:01:35.767 --> 00:01:39.598
Initialization runs the static initializer, <clinit>.

32
00:01:39.598 --> 00:01:44.874
That is every static field initializer and static block, in source order.

33
00:01:44.874 --> 00:01:48.416
CAP becomes 500,000, and the static block prints.

34
00:01:48.633 --> 00:01:50.667
Only this phase runs your code.

35
00:01:51.467 --> 00:01:59.729
It starts on a new instance, a static method call, a static field write, or a read of a static field that is not a constant.

36
00:01:59.933 --> 00:02:02.059
A constant read doesn't start it.

37
00:02:02.059 --> 00:02:05.216
Neither does Limits.class, or an array of Limits.

38
00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:09.819
Check: which of these two reads runs the static initializer?

39
00:02:10.033 --> 00:02:11.725
MAX_MINOR is a constant.

40
00:02:11.725 --> 00:02:16.447
javac copied its value into your class, so reading it runs nothing.

41
00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:21.461
CAP is a boxed Integer, not a constant, so reading it runs <clinit>.

42
00:02:21.461 --> 00:02:25.128
Kotlin's const val and plain val split the same way.
